---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import StatusBadge from "../../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import { studies, studyPath, STATUS_LABEL, STATUS_HELP, SPLIT_LABEL } from "../../lib/data";

const ws = Object.fromEntries((await getCollection("workstreams")).map((w) => [w.data.id, w.data]));
const all = await studies();
const n = (id: string) => all.filter((s) => s.data.workstream === id).length;
const card = (id: string) => ({ id, href: `/research/${id.toLowerCase()}/`, ...ws[id], count: n(id) });
const stages = [[card("W0")], [card("W1")], [card("W2")], [card("W3"), card("W4")], [card("W5")]];

// "How we keep records": the status vocabulary, and one real study record annotated with the rules.
const ORDER = ["closed-positive", "closed-negative", "closed-inconclusive", "running", "planned", "abandoned"];
const example = all.find((s) => s.data.id === "cifar10-repro") ?? all.find((s) => s.data.status.startsWith("closed"));
const ex = example?.data;
const kr = ex?.key_results?.[0];
const num = (v: number) => v.toFixed(2).replace(/^-/, "−");
---
<Base title="Research" description="How the NeuroPulse project fits together: six workstreams, from reproducing ASF-S to continual learning.">
  <div class="container">
    <section class="section">
      <h1>How the project fits together</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Our project has six workstreams. Each one answers a question the next one depends on. We first had to check that the pruning method we build on
        does what its paper reports (W1). Then we asked whether its central idea holds up under a fair comparison (W2), and only then whether it helps
        a network learn many tasks (W3 and W4).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The six workstreams</h2>
      <div class="pipeline">
        {stages.map((stage) => (
          <div class="stage">
            <div class="stack">
              {stage.map((w) => (
                <a class="card" href={w.href}>
                  <span class="ws-tag">{w.id}</span>
                  <h3 style="margin-top:6px">{w.title}</h3>
                  <p class="muted small">{w.tagline}</p>
                  <p class="small" style="margin:0">{w.count === 0 ? "No studies yet" : `${w.count} ${w.count === 1 ? "study" : "studies"}`}</p>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>The idea we are testing, in six steps</h2>
      <p>
        Artificial Structure Function Search (ASF-S) is a way of deciding which convolutional filters a network can lose. Most pruning methods remove the
        filters with the smallest weights. ASF-S instead looks at how the network's output units behave together, and keeps the filters that shape
        that behaviour. This is our own summary of the method. The <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a> explains each term.
      </p>
    </section>
    <ol class="flow">
      <li><strong>Collect responses</strong><span>Show the trained network many images, and record its output-layer activations separately for images it classifies correctly and incorrectly.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Build connectivity graphs</strong><span>For each group, connect output units that respond similarly. This graph is the Artificial Functional Connectivity (AFC).</span></li>
      <li><strong>Find the main gradient</strong><span>A diffusion-map analysis orders the units along the graph's main axis of variation, the first principal gradient (PG1).</span></li>
      <li><strong>Contrast right and wrong</strong><span>Subtract the "incorrect" gradient from the "correct" one, and summarise the difference with principal component analysis.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Score every filter</strong><span>Principal Gradient Importance (PGI) rates each filter by how strongly its weights align with that difference.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Prune and recover</strong><span>Remove the lowest-scoring filters, then retrain only the final layer. The paper's claim is that little accuracy is lost.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <section class="section prose">
      <p>
        Step 5 is where our work found the most trouble. As written, the formula multiplies vectors of different lengths, and the original code resolves
        this in a way that makes the score close to random. The details are in the <a href="/research/w1/">reproduction studies</a>. Our corrected version
        is the one we test in <a href="/research/w2/">W2</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="records" aria-labelledby="records-title">
      <h2 id="records-title">How we keep records</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Every experiment belongs to a study: one question, a hypothesis, and a success criterion. When a study ends, it gets one of six statuses,
        and we report the ones that did not hold as fully as the ones that did.
      </p>
      <dl class="legend" aria-label="What each status means">
        {ORDER.map((st) => (
          <div><dt><span class={`status status-${st}`}>{STATUS_LABEL[st]}</span></dt><dd>{STATUS_HELP[st]}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="legend-note">
        <strong>Pre-registered</strong> means the success criterion was written down before any results existed; <strong>post hoc</strong> means after,
        and we label it so. Every number says whether it comes from validation or test data, and how many seeds (independent training runs) it averages.
      </p>

      {ex && example && (
        <>
          <h3>One real record, annotated</h3>
          <p class="muted">This is the record of our CIFAR-10 reproduction, as it appears on its study page. The numbers point to the rules on the right.</p>
          <div class="record-wrap">
            <dl class="record">
              <div><dt>Question<sup>1</sup></dt><dd>{ex.question}</dd></div>
              <div>
                <dt>Criterion<sup>1</sup></dt>
                <dd>
                  {ex.criterion === "pre_registered" ? "Pre-registered: written down before any results existed." : "Post hoc: written or revised after results existed, and labelled as such."}
                  <span class="muted">The full success criterion is on the study page.</span>
                </dd>
              </div>
              {kr && (
                <div>
                  <dt>Headline number<sup>2</sup></dt>
                  <dd class="record-result">
                    <strong>{num(kr.value)}{kr.std !== undefined && ` ± ${kr.std.toFixed(2)}`}</strong> {kr.unit}
                    {kr.compare && <> against <strong>{num(kr.compare.value)}</strong> ({kr.compare.label})</>}
                    <span class="muted">{kr.label}. Measured on {SPLIT_LABEL[kr.split]}, {kr.n_seeds} {kr.n_seeds === 1 ? "seed" : "seeds"}.</span>
                  </dd>
                </div>
              )}
              {ex.verdict && <div><dt>Verdict<sup>3</sup></dt><dd><StatusBadge status={ex.status} /> <span class="muted">{ex.verdict}</span></dd></div>}
              <div><dt>Code and records<sup>2</sup></dt><dd><a href={ex.code_url}>Code, configuration and lab log on GitHub</a>. <a href={studyPath(example)}>Open the full study page</a>.</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <ol class="principles">
              <li><h4>One question per study</h4><p>Every experiment belongs to a study with a question, a hypothesis and a success criterion, and we say whether that criterion came before or after the results.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Every number has a source</h4><p>Each result says whether it comes from validation or test data and how many seeds it averages. Each run records its code version and settings.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Negative results count</h4><p>When something does not reproduce, we publish it with the same care, and separate what we ruled out from what we only suspect.</p></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </>
      )}
      <p class="muted small">
        The full rules are in our <a href="https://github.com/NeuroPulse-Co/.github/blob/main/standards/RECORDS.md">record standard</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
